    id: 'kyodai-on-mittens',
    title: 'On Mittens',
    level: '難関大（評論）',
    paragraphs: [
      "Every winter, on the railings beside park paths, a small sad object appears: a single child's mitten, placed there by some kind stranger in the hope that its owner will come back for it. I have never seen anyone collect one of these mittens, and I suspect that most of them stay where they are until spring. Yet the sight always moves me a little, partly because the mitten so clearly belongs to someone very small, and partly because it is useless without its partner.",
      "The mitten is one of the simplest pieces of clothing ever made. Unlike a glove, it does not divide the hand into five separate parts; it gives the thumb a room of its own and puts the four fingers together in a single larger one. This arrangement has a practical advantage, since fingers that share a space can share their warmth, and for this reason mittens are generally thought to be warmer than gloves of the same material. The price of this warmth, however, is clumsiness. Anyone wearing mittens soon discovers how hard it is to tie a shoelace, open a zip or turn a single page.",
      "In this sense, the mitten represents a choice that we face in many areas of life, a choice between protection and skill. A glove allows the hand to remain clever, but leaves each finger alone in the cold. A mitten keeps the hand warm and comfortable, but asks it to give up, for a while, most of the delicate things it knows how to do. It is no accident, I think, that mittens are linked above all with young children, who are not yet expected to do delicate things. They are also linked with people in the coldest places, who cannot afford to lose warmth for the sake of skill.",
      "The familiar string that connects a child's two mittens through the sleeves of a coat is a small admission of the mitten's other weakness. Because a mitten is so loose and so easy to pull off, it is also very easy to lose. Parents who thread such a string through a coat are not thinking about philosophy, but they are solving a real problem: they are making sure that the two halves of a pair cannot be ( A ). The string turns two objects into one, and in doing so it protects the mittens from the carelessness of their owner.",
      "I remember the winter when I was finally allowed to wear gloves instead. It felt like a promotion, a sign that I was now trusted to manage my own fingers and to keep track of my own belongings. Most of us make this change somewhere in childhood, and few of us go back. We tell ourselves that gloves are more practical, and in many situations they certainly are. But I wonder whether part of the reason is simply that mittens make us look, and feel, younger than we wish to be.",
      "Recently, on a very cold morning, I bought a pair of mittens for the first time in decades. The first few minutes were frustrating, since I could not find my keys in my pocket. After a while, though, I noticed something unexpected. Because my hands could do so little, I stopped trying to make them do anything at all, and simply walked on with my hands warm and still, looking at the frost on the grass. The mittens had not only kept me warm; they had also, in a small way, released me from the habit of being busy.",
      "I do not want to make too much of this. Gloves are useful, and nobody would choose to cook or to write a letter while wearing mittens. Yet the mitten reminds me that warmth and skill are not always available at the same time, and that there may be moments when it is wiser to choose warmth. The lonely mitten on the park railing, waiting for an owner who has probably forgotten it, seems to me a quiet reminder of this lesson.",
    ],
